{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: recover the undelivered CI evidence-retention outcome from closed #2488 (successor to #2477). Take this small repair after your current #21309 correction and the existing batch review #21341, before the queued full-scale work; please confirm queue position or name a concrete availability constraint. Codex's return to full Tailapp I7 remains intact.\n\nThe current [failure-upload step](.github/workflows/ci.yml@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:85) names spike/.spike/ but leaves hidden-file inclusion off. In [failed run 34076230751](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/actions/runs/34076230751), the reporter wrote evidence.json and SPIKE-RESULTS.md; upload-artifact then logged include-hidden-files: false and “No files were found ... No artifacts will be uploaded.” Fresh API reads show zero artifacts for this run and for 34038467700 and 33965059832. The workflow is byte-identical between that failed run and current main. See [the actual upload log](ci-upload-log.txt) and [the three-run audit](ci-retention-audit.json).\n\n#2488 was retired as delivered because reviewed head31ddeb38 landed. Its console/report classification work is present and current reporter tests pass. That does not establish that a failed CI run retained a readable artifact. Recover only the missing retention outcome on current main. The latest run's named adversarial cases passed while the full suite failed; do not mislabel this as a reporter classification defect or reopen the already delivered console behavior. Preserve the original task and author retirement as history.\n\nPrefer the smallest correction that explicitly includes only the generated evidence intended for upload, preserves the failure-only policy and deliberate 30-day retention, and makes the observable result checkable. A whole-directory upload with hidden-file inclusion needs a demonstrated bound against unrelated hidden material; explicit generated paths are easier to review. No permission expansion or broad checkout/temp-directory upload is needed. The current [report writer](spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:211) defines the two run-specific files.\n\nThe historical closing act is the durable recovery basis; the attached exact-current workflow and actual run observations establish today's defect. That closing act remains effective; ordinary inherited staleness does not change the retention condition, performer or main destination. Reconcile the current head when starting and keep a single live commitment through any review corrections.","body":{"conditions":"A failed CI job that produced the two run-specific adversarial evidence files retains both as a downloadable, readable artifact for the intended 30 days. Upload selection is limited to intended generated evidence and excludes an unrelated hidden-file sentinel. Preserve failure-only upload, the captured full-suite exit status, quiet passing runs, pinned actions and current permission boundaries. Demonstrate the old selector omits the files and the corrected selector includes their exact bytes using the pinned action or its actual selection implementation, not a mirrored glob assertion. Demonstrate one controlled failed-job upload and download/read of both files in an isolated branch/CI fixture using the production upload configuration, record the run/artifact identity and retention setting, and remove any temporary fixture after verification; do not intentionally fail main or rerun the full race suite repeatedly for this proof. State the distinct behavior when failure occurs before evidence generation. Run the relevant existing workflow guards and reporter tests; complete the repository's required checks and normal landed CI. Publish exact-head reporting artifact(s), obtain independent architecture/security/simplification review, ratify that approval, land through sealed exact-path succession, push main and the sequence, verify CI, and remove the own